also prove a version of our signed e-expansion for the\nchromatic symmetric function for arbitrary graphs.\nKeywords. Chromatic quasisymmetric function, elementary symmetric function, natural\nunit interval graph, proper colouring, Shareshian–Wachs conjecture, Stanley–Stembridge\nconjecture\nMathematics Subject Classifications. 05E05, 05E10, 05C15\n1. Introduction\nIn 1995, Stanley [Sta95] introduced the chromatic symmetric function XG(x), a generaliza-\ntion of the chromatic polynomial of a graph G, and famously conjectured with Stembridge\n[Sta95, SS93] that XG(x) is e-positive whenever G is the incomparability graph of a (3 + 1)-free\npartially ordered set. Guay-Paquet [GP13] showed that it suffices to prove e-positivity for the\nsmaller class of natural unit interval graphs. Shareshian and Wachs [SW16] defined a fur-\nther generalization called the chromatic quasisymmetric function XG(x; q), showed that if G\nis a natural unit interval graph, then XG(x; q) is in fact symmetric; and conjectured that it is\ne-positive and e-unimodal for such graphs. This notorious problem has received considerable\nattention. Many authors proved e-positivity for certain families of graphs [AWvW24, CH22,\nCH19, CMP23, Dah18, DvW18, Ell17, FHM19, HNY20, LY21, MPW24, Wan25, WW23,\nWan22, Zhe22], studied related positivity problems [Ath15, BEPS25, CZ22, DRS25, DFvW20,\nDSvW21, DSvW20, DvW20, Gas96, GP13, Pau16, RS23, Sie22], and explored alternative gen-\neralizations of the chromatic symmetric function [AN21, ALvW24, APCSZ21, CHM24, CPS23,\nCS20, GS01, Sta98]. The Stanley–Stembridge conjecture is also closely related to positivity of--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChromatic quasisymmetric functions | SymCat (https://www.symmetricfunctions.com/chromaticQuasisymmetric.htm)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Salois study when a chromatic quasisymmetric function is symmetric [GPS24]. ... One variant normalizes over all ve